\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar {\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8206\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx8209\dppty1\dpx216\dpy130\dpxsize8209\dpysize1\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20}}{\b\i 0
\par }\pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\tx360\tx900\tx1440\tx2070\tx2520\tx2880\tx3330\tx3960\tx4500\tx5130\tx5760\tx6390 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267963}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc404402571}\tab A \tab M \tab J \tab J \tab A \tab S \tab O \tab N \tab D \tab J \tab 
F \tab M{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267963}{\*\bkmkend _Toc404402571} 
\par \pard\plain \s5\qc\keepn\widctlpar \b\f40\lang2057 MONTHS
\par \pard\plain \qj\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 {\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8347\dprect\dpx72\dpy0\dpxsize289\dpysize289\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr0\dpfillbgcg0\dpfillbgcb255\dpfillpat1}}{\b \tab  TOTAL
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar {\b\i 
\par }\pard \qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\tx1980 {\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8354\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx1297\dppty1\dpx72\dpy96\dpxsize1297\dpysize1\dplinesolid\dplinecor255\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew10}}{\b  \tab 1992-1993 AVERAGE
\par }\pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\b Root cause analysis:
\par }\pard \qj\li720\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar (1) Class 158 bogies paint problems
\par (2) Class 158 bogies unsatisfactory overhaul
\par (3) Class 37 LOCO Noise Modification
\par (4) Class 47 LOCO AVR power fault
\par (5) 58 LOCO 
\par A - failed inlet T piece guide
\par B - exhaust valve bent
\par C - Suspect piston crown damage
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\b \line }As was mentioned above, even after implementation of the TQM policy there were some delays in the repair of vehicles, caused by several reasons, including t
he shortage of material, and as a consequence of the shortages of spare parts - a low level of quality of repair. But also the quality of repair depended not only on external factors, as a lack of spare parts, but also on the{\i  people\rquote s} {\i 
mind,}
 their awareness of the processes of quality. And here we could see that not every person on the shop floor was conscious about quality things. The level of culture on the shop floor needed to be changed. But it could not happen overnight. People had to h
ave some time for changing their mind in terms of safety and to broaden their scope. Not everybody could do it. Some of them still tried to put aside the basic things:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 If a situation arises on the job that we don\rquote t agree with then we will argue against it with the chargehand. Sometimes the trouble is caused by men wanting {\ul 
to cut corners in order to complete the job quicker}, {\ul putting aside Health and Safety Regulations}. These people are classed as bad tradesmen who often cannot be trusted and whose work has to be double-check
ed all the time. If the trouble is down to bad workmanship then the person responsible is disciplined by the foreman.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
So, there were people on the shop floor, who wanted to cut corners, do short cuts, or speed up their work at the expense of quality. Within the plant a number of control practices were used, which can operate in a partly autonomous manner. These practices
 were built into the working process, it was a necessary part of the technological procedure: there was a day-to-day control of \lquote who does what, where and when\rquote . And there was a procedure to discipline someone who \lquote steps out of line
\rquote {\i .}
\par At the beginning of each shift the foremen will tell the chargehand the amount of work to do. The chargehand will allocate the job among the gang. The foreman was responsible for getting the work done on time. Also the foreman and chargehand controlled th
e work - the workload of the workers during the day, the quality and so on. Besides, there was a whole procedure of organising paperwork, which helped the chargehand to control the work of his gang:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 
When a door is regulated with the striking plate I then sign a list to say it is fitted and working correctly. At the end of the shift, the list is returned to the chargehand. When the job is completed the list is photo-copied and the chargehand and forem
an keep a copy. If there is a problem with a door due to bad workmanship, the list is checked and the offender is disciplined. I always double-check my doors making sure that everything is done properly in accordance wit
h the regulations (Extract from the diary of the working week. 1994).
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 So within the plant a system of technological control was organised. The offenders could be fined and disciplined:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 Wednesday 30-th March, 1994. Wooden brigade.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
My job today is to fix the bodyside mouldings while the other three men fit the draught proofing and casings around the doors. Throughout the night five men are taken into the office over bad workmanship. The reasons were really down to mistakes made duri
ng the job so they were only minor offences, therefore the men were let off.
\par \pard\plain \s21\qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Disciplinary procedure for bad workmanship or other offences had several stages:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 1.\tab}\pard\plain \qj\fi-360\li720\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\tx720{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\f40\lang2057 Some form of counselling
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 2.\tab}Verbal warning.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 3.\tab}Verbal notification.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 4.\tab}Written notification. Disciplinary procedure. (Suspension).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 5.\tab}Sack.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
The regulations according to which all the work should correspond had been introduced several years ago, but it was only recently that people had begun to work according to these regulations. As one of the workers pointed out:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 We have an official list o
f instructions. Now we have to read it. A few years ago - we never read one of the job instructions. Now - in order to get a quality assurance - you should realise that you\rquote ve got to do it (Interview with worker, March 1994).
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
The most important thing for the plant was that in 1989 the Doncaster plant adopted British Rail standard 5750 (British Standard Quality System). In order to meet all the requirements of it all the staff should be quality conscious. As the workers replied
 about it:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 Everybody became fanatical about safety. We\rquote ve been on safety courses. We\rquote ve got very many certificates, including environmental standards.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar All these safety factors will be prioritised.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
Adopting this quality standard meant that the senior managers became responsible for quality policy within the plant. There should be a whole system of quality and management representatives had to check quality. The new system of document control was bro
ught in. Besides there should be a process of control of basic requirements that the job sh
ould be done correctly. Inspection and testing of the process and product. Moreover, the management representatives had to test and keep records of any tests they performed. According to these systems, within the plant a whole systems of corrective action
 should be organised as an important element of British Standard. It was constructed in a such way that if anything goes wrong - it was possible to investigate and take action to prevent it from happening again. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar But of course the commitment to quality coul
d not happen suddenly after the adoption of this new system. And the adoption of this system generated some problems on the shop floor. One of them was signing for your own work. After adopting the new system people were not forced to sign for their own w
ork, but it was recommendable. What was happening in reality is clear from the words of the General Manager:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 There were a number of things that arose. What I\rquote ve got there, the practice of people signing for their own work was not universally accepted throug
h the works. You see my idea on those things was \lquote Okay, that\rquote s a state of mind.\rquote  And as far as possible there was \lquote no blame culture\rquote 
. And no blame culture is a very very difficult thing to do, you know. Why it is so contradictory, because what you are try
ing to do - is that you are responsible - it is a very subtle set of thoughts. I am blaming you that you are responsible. One contradicts the other. What you are trying to do is to preach responsibility: yeah, that\rquote 
s my word. There is my name against it. So that is my guarantee that it\rquote s good. Of course none of us are one hundred percenters, so there is a problem, there is an element of fear, and I think that\rquote s what motivated people to say \lquote 
We don\rquote t want to do it\rquote . What are you going to do if we get it wrong?\rquote  People want to know, if...
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The problem of signing for their own job was not resolved simultaneously. Moreover, from time to time some disputes on this matter arose:{\b  
\par }\pard\plain \s4\qj\sb120\sa60\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\i\f40\lang2057  {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267964}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554681}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378646}\lquote Who does what?\rquote  dispute on the second bay. March 1995.
{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267964}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554681}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378646} 
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 When BREL was transformed int
o BRML in 1987 - from the first day all the examiners were removed from the structure. The initial repair of a vehicle is very difficult and responsible work, when the aged loco comes on the works it has to be initially checked to find out if the normal o
r abnormal repair should be done. This involves an extensive inspection, which could last for 2-3 days. There are 15 pages of explanations about the correct things inside the loco. At the end the examiner should write a report, which could cost the client
 company thousands of pounds.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
The policy of the company after elimination of the examiners was that it is the duty of Cat4 staff. But these duties were not clearly written or agreed. Cat4 electricians had wanted some form of recognition ever since 1987 because they haven\rquote 
t got any extra pay for this official duties.
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 It has always been a little bit of a grey area (Interview with a production manager. March 1995).
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The production initiative made staff (fitters, electricians, and managers) even more flexible, demanding more awareness about quality and delivery. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar There was a \lquote good will basis\rquote 
 working in relation to the customers. Five or six of the electricians have done this work since 1987. They checked all the vehicle inside, underneath - all electrical repairs. From the managerial point of view there are certa
in duties which are Cat 4 work - final setting, testing and signing off, and this was not negotiable \endash  \lquote It is what we pay you for\rquote  \endash  but the pre-examination was a grey area. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Recently the rolling stock beca
me part of different private companies. The different owners have different specifications. The process of examining became much more complicated than it was before, besides they should do a technical report. The process of writing it sometimes takes two 
or three days, there are a lot of details, too much time, and a very high level of responsibility. The electricians made a protest against such a situation. It was an informal protest, which caused a lot of friction on the shop floor. But, according to on
e of the workers on the shop floor:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 They continued to work at the end of the day in order to please the master.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
As a result of these frictions a serious Quality Improvement Team Meeting was held, where 6 representatives of managers and 6 electricians took part. As a result of the negotiations a consensus was reached and people voted for it. The main points of this 
agreement were:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 1.\tab}\pard \qj\fi-360\li720\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\tx720{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}The responsibility of examining among the electricians will continue to be fulfilled by Cat4 workers.

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 2.\tab}\pard \qj\fi-360\li720\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\tx720{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}More training should be provided for all of them.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 3.\tab}Documents for examination should be easier to read.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 4.\tab}The small number of the examiners should be increased.
\par \pard\plain \s21\qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
In accordance with these agreements, 6 weeks before 10-15 people were upgraded or reclassified. 10 extra chargehands were appointed to do examining. The company said that it was the responsibility of the Category 4, because they\rquote 
ve done it for the last 5 - 6 years. It should be very qualified men who should do a very responsible job. 
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 But the success of the electricians had a \lquote copy-cat\rquote 
 effect. Workers from the other trades wanted the same things as the electricians. Throughout the factory there was a disturbance on this matter. 
\par These things were misinterpreted on the shop floor. There it was regarded as a \lquote management manoeuvre\rquote . The workers thought that \lquote once again the supervisors are rewarded and we didn\rquote t get any particular pay for it\rquote . 

\par \pard\plain \s21\qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Many people on the shop floor thought that they keep taking on work which is not rewarded at all. 
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Once on the afternoon shift two{\b\i  }fitters were asked to check the door on one of the trains. The two fitters would do the door check, but they wouldn\rquote 
t record it - they were advised to do it by the trade union. The chargehand (Grade 4) had to sign for it.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar  
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 We would expect to do some measurement fitted to the client, but they refused. Four individuals were personally involved in this dispute.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Managers didn\rquote t want to wash their dirty linen in public, which is why they were trying to do everything possible to prevent the conflict from being transferred to the next level. 
\par \pard\plain \s21\qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 How the things developed is clear from the next passage:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 21.3.95
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\b\i Production manager
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\i Industrial Relations problem 2 Bay
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar At approximately 19.35 hours this evening, I was contacted at home Shift Production Manager, who informed me that Fitter {\ul N}
 was refusing to complete the recording of documentation - complete c6 Door Check.
\par I suggested to the Shift Production Manager that he spoke with the fitter and reiterated to him that Management considered it was a reasonable instruction and the work was considered by management to be a Cat 4 duty.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Fitter informed Shift Production Manager that he had sought advice from his Union Representative who had stated the Depot Council considered the duty of filling in any documentation was a chargehand
\rquote s responsibility and should only be done by a Cat. 4 on voluntary basis. He again stated he was refusing to fill in documentation.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar An approximately 20:00 hours, I attended work and reviewed the situation with Shift Production Manager.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar I asked another manager to speak with fitters {\ul N} and {\ul M}
 and advise them to complete documentation. If they were to refuse, they would be considered to be refusing a reasonable work instruction and would be invited to explain their position to Production manager.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar The two fitters {\ul N }and {\ul M} again refused the work instruction from their Supervisor and were sent to myself and Shift Production Manager.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar I strongly advised them both to carry out the work instruction or they would find themselves in serious trouble. I explained that I considered the 
work instruction was a reasonable request and that they should carry out the work even if it was recorded they were conducting it under protest subject to the Depot Council meeting Management the following morning.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\ul K} and {\ul L}
, the leaders of the trade union, arrived at the Depot and were invited to attend the meeting. Discussion continued in exactly the same vein as the informal meeting between Management and the Deputy Council the previous Friday.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar After discussion, I saw no alternative but to reiterate Management\rquote s position. I asked the fitters {\ul N} and {\ul M}
 to reconsider and to carry out the work instruction (even under protest) which I considered to be a reasonable request. Failure to comply would be viewed seriously and would result in both individuals being dealt with under Disciplinary Procedure.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar I stated this would be a very regrettable course of action from Management but I would have no alternative considering the overall situation and course of events.
\par Again, both staff refused the work instruction and I informed them they would be dealt with trough Disciplinary Procedure.
\par I invited the trade union leaders {\ul K} and {\ul L} to attend a meeting with Depot Manager at 9:00 Wednesday morning.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
At approximately 21.40 hours I was contacted at home by one of the supervisors, who informed me that two others fitters O and P had returned a Brake Test Sheet refusing to complete the documentation and recording of measurement taken etc.
\par I asked manager to inform fitters that failure to complete the documentation was to refuse a reasonable work instruction and statutory requirement. Again both staff refused and they were told they would be dealt with through the Disciplinary Procedure.

\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar I instructed 2 Bay Supervisors at approximately 21.50 that the offer of overtime working was withdrawn from the DMU/coaching department until the situation was resolved.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Production manager.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 This dispute shows that to be a manager at this particular plant means \lquote to do a lot of informal bargaining\rquote  if there is a disturbance - but it is not a formal procedur
e. This dispute also shows that there was the possibility for some overt resistance - that meant that people could express their disagreement and that they were not afraid to do it. With privatisation, as we will see, all the overt expression became laten
t. And the final resolution of this dispute, following privatisation, shows this well. The staff are now forced to do the examining in, but without any additional money. After the acquisition by Adtranz within new terms and conditions, staff have to sign 
off their own work, do the examining in and lots of other things. All the waged staff signed new terms and conditions in July 1996. The salaried staff signed new terms and conditions in September 1996.
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267965}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554682}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378647}The Fundamental Change Initiative Team (FCIT){\*\bkmkend _Toc404267965}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554682}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378647} 
\par \pard\plain \s21\qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 In February 1994 the \lquote Fundamental Change Initiative Team\rquote 
 (FCIT) was organised. The B.R.M.L. board wanted to find out the areas, where there could be quick savings across the site. The aim of this Team was
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 to develop and expedite the changes required to reshape B.R
.M.L. into a competitive commercially effective company. The driving force behind the initiative is the Privatisation Process; the Initiative aims to anticipate the changes that would be forced upon B.R.M.L./Level 5 sites by new owners, with a view to imp
lementing these changes whilst the opportunity still exists to do so on our own terms. It is hoped that an early implementation of these changes may make the sites more attractive to prospective purchasers, and will also demonstrate to new owners that the
 sites are capable of developing and managing the change process.{\cs16\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s22\qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs20\lang2057 {\cs16\super \chftn } Doncaster Fundamental Change Initiative proposals. Introduction. page 1.}}

\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The following restrictions have applied to the changes proposed by FCIT:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 1.\tab}\pard \qj\fi-288\li1368\sb60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\tx1080{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxta .}}No changes to grading structures
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 2.\tab}\pard \qj\fi-288\li1368\sb60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\tx1080{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxta .}}No changes to conditions of service, methods of payment, hours of duty, and allowances.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f40 3.\tab}\pard \qj\fi-288\li1368\sb60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\tx1080{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent283\pnhang{\pntxta .}}No changes to work skill levels.
\par \pard \qj\li1080\sb60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\tx1080 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar At Doncaster it was established as a team of four Management/Supervisory staff working in isolation from their normal duties. The team had a remit from the Depot manager to investigate any aspect of the Depot
\rquote s functions. 
\par \pard\plain \s5\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f40\lang2057 Current Culture according to FCIT
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
Information was obtained from all departments on site. The Management team was interviewed on their personal responsibilities and the role of their teams. The representatives of FCIT gave examples of the perceptions and attitudes that the change team beli
eves to be prevalent on the site. It is revealed below:{\cs16\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s22\qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs20\lang2057 {\cs16\super \chftn } Doncaster Fundamental Change Initiative proposals. Introduction. page 9.}}
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 EVERYBODY\rquote S PERCEPTION OF THE CURRENT CULTURE ACCORDING TO FCIT
\par \pard \s18\qj\li1440\sb60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar * I do as little as possible for as much as possible.
\par * We get paid attendance money.
\par * I only use 5% of my brain, because that\rquote s all I\rquote m expected to use.
\par * I\rquote m only a \lquote rubbing rag\rquote .
\par * I don\rquote t get paid for thinking.
\par * Nobody thanks you for trying.
\par * I only come for the money.
\par * I only come out of necessity
\par * A fair day\rquote s pay for a fair day\rquote s work.
\par * It makes no difference what I do, so why bother.
\par * Better than being at Maplins Holiday Camp.
\par * I do the work of six men.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li1440\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar * We reward poor productivity with overtime.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\ul B.R. Overtime perception:
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Traditionally the pay structure has been operated on a historical
 basis, the base rate system with additions that apply under set of conditions has been with us for many years and most people see this system as antiquated but useful in certain situations.
\par On pay rise calculations, the % increase applied on base rate figures is enlarged compared to high day rate figures. But by far the most significant psychological effect is the fact that overtime is used as a \lquote satisfying medium\rquote 
 to enable staff to live well, compared to just managing.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The Fundamental Change Initiative Team investigated ineffective time (also referred to as \lquote Lost Time\rquote ) in the workshops. They defined \lquote ineffective time\rquote 
 as that part of the working day where no work related activity occurs. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar They gave examples of ineffective time:
\par \pard \qj\li1440\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar * Beginning/end of the shift
\par * Before/after meal breaks
\par \pard \qj\li1440\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar * Random unauthorised breaks during the shift.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar A typical shift in March 1994 is shown in the following \lquote Bar Chart\rquote  
\par \pard \qr\sa120\widctlpar {\b Chart 1}
\par \pard \qc\sa120\widctlpar {\b Typical eight hour shift 
\par }\pard \qj\sa120\widctlpar {\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8479\dprect\dpx6840\dpy90\dpxsize577\dpysize865\dplinesolid\dplinecor255\dplinecog255\dplinecob0\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr0\dpfillbgcg0\dpfillbgcb255\dpfillpat1}{\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8475\dprect\dpx1512\dpy88\dpxsize433\dpysize865\dplinesolid\dplinecor255\dplinecog255\dplinecob0\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr0\dpfillbgcg0\dpfillbgcb255\dpfillpat1}{\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8474\dprect\dpx216\dpy88\dpxsize576\dpysize865\dplinesolid\dplinecor255\dplinecog255\dplinecob0\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr0\dpfillbgcg0\dpfillbgcb255\dpfillpat1}{\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8476\dprect\dpx2952\dpy90\dpxsize577\dpysize865\dplinesolid\dplinecor255\dplinecog255\dplinecob0\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr0\dpfillbgcg0\dpfillbgcb255\dpfillpat1}{\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8484\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx1\dppty865\dpx7416\dpy90\dpxsize1\dpysize865\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20
}{\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8483\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx1\dppty865\dpx216\dpy90\dpxsize1\dpysize865\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20}{\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8482\dprect\dpx3528\dpy90\dpxsize433\dpysize865
\dplinesolid\dplinecor255\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr217\dpfillbgcg217\dpfillbgcb217\dpfillpat1}{\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8481\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx7201\dppty1
\dpx216\dpy90\dpxsize7201\dpysize1\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20}{\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8478\dprect\dpx5544\dpy90\dpxsize433\dpysize865\dplinesolid\dplinecor255\dplinecog255\dplinecob0\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr0\dpfillbgcg0\dpfillbgcb255\dpfillpat1}{\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8477\dprect\dpx3960\dpy90\dpxsize433\dpysize865\dplinesolid\dplinecor255\dplinecog255\dplinecob0\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr0\dpfillbgcg0\dpfillbgcb255\dpfillpat1}}
\par {\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8480\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx7201\dppty1\dpx216\dpy99\dpxsize7201\dpysize1\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20}}
\par \pard \qj\sa120\widctlpar\tx450\tx1710\tx3240\tx4140\tx5760\tx7110 
\par \tab 25 \tab 10 \tab 20 \tab 10 \tab 10 \tab 30
\par \pard \qj\widctlpar\tx450\tx1710\tx3240\tx4140\tx5760\tx7110 \tab mins.\tab  mins. \tab mins. \tab mins. \tab mins. \tab mins.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\widctlpar {\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8485\dprect\dpx216\dpy252\dpxsize433\dpysize433\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob255\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr0\dpfillbgcg0\dpfillbgcb255\dpfillpat1}}
\par \pard \qj\fi720\li720\sa120\widctlpar Ineffective time, estimated at 1 hour and 45 min.
\par \pard \qj\sb120\widctlpar {\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8486\dprect\dpx216\dpy120\dpxsize433\dpysize433\dplinesolid\dplinecor255\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr217\dpfillbgcg217\dpfillbgcb217\dpfillpat1}}\tab \tab Official meal break - 20 min.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
According to the bar chart, shown above, ineffective time mainly occurs at the beginning and at the end of the shifts, and at the beginning and end of the meal break. At this time the workers used to have a chat with the chaps, having their coffee, tea, m
ake something for themselves and so on.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Despite ineffective time, which employees spend on activity which does not relate to their work, there was a time, defined as an
cillary time - time during the working day that is spent on support activities associated with work content time. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar The Change team identified those ancillaries that had potential for change and they are shown as follows:
\par \pard \qj\li720\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar * Search for material
\par * Waiting for material
\par * Discuss job.
\par * Wait for a vehicle movement.
\par * Give/receive/wait for instructions.
\par * Wait for crane
\par * Obtain tools and equipment
\par \pard \qj\li720\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar * Visit to stores. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar The results of their investigation were presented as a report to the British board. The FCIT offered to reduce overheads with the help of a reduction of spare time, move towards a tighter work process and so on. 

\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Despite the professional efforts of the FCIT, nevertheless, the work of this team was not seen as important on the shop floor. Moreover, the work of this team didn\rquote 
t have very much influence on the shop floor. Some supervisors were very sceptical about the real importance of their work. As regards the workers, they seemed to know nothing about this team. The Trade Union leaders considered them as a \lquote 
team of traitors\rquote , which was{\i  }
searching for imperfections on the shop floor. And even top managers regarded them as a team imposed by the British Railway board, whom the plant should obey. The team worked for one year and at the end of their work all the people involved returned to th
eir previous positions. The team did not have any influence on the production process within the enterprise.
\par \pard\plain \s2\sb360\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\i\f5\fs28\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267966}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554683}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378648}Conclusion{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267966}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554683}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378648}

\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 This section has revealed that formal and informal relations are interrelated. The informal relations covered a lot of processes within the enterprise \endash 
 the system of informal relations penetrated the whole industrial process. The relations between workers, managers and middle managers were regulated through the system of unwritten regulations. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
The other observation is that, as a rule, those who use the system of informal relations benefit from it. The workers, if they know and use these unwritten rules, can get easier overtime, the managers within the informal system search for ways of promotio
n and so on. There is a Russian saying that \lquote the fish is looking for a deeper place, but the man for a better one\rquote . That is to say, people choose the {\ul most profitable way of doing things}
. In our case it is more profitable to use the system of informal relations rather than the formal. And such stability of informal relations is that in any circumstances it is profitable for somebody to use it. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar As regards the variety of informal relations and the levels of informality, we have noticed the whole range of relations from very informal ways of promotion to half-formal ways of relating to customers.
\par The willingness of people to use these relations reveals another key issue \endash 
 people are interested in the resolution of existing problems. That is why they are looking for any possible way of resolving their problems with a mutually advantageous approach.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar The analysis of the literature and out own observation allow us to conclude that all these kinds of relations exist in every society and that they penetrate all societal levels.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
Despite the system of formal control on the shop floor, a lot of processes within the shop floor at Doncaster were handled in an informal way. The system of informal relations began from the relations with your boss. The whole atmosphere in the workshop w
as very informal. It was confirmed in many interviews:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 
Everybody is on first name terms with the Management, who will always acknowledge you when passing. If you feel that you have a problem with anything, whether it is personal or it concerns the job, you are always made welcome to discuss the situation.

\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 As the data from the plant revealed, the formal structure of control did not always work and the informal structure sometimes were more hidden, but at the same time were more active.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
So, these two systems had a parallel existence on the shop floor, but all the parties involved preferred the use of informal means. And if in theory things had to go through the system of formal norms and regulations, in reality it was a much more complic
ated process. In the s
ystem where production played the decisive role in industrial processes, some things like, for example, quality, had minor importance. As the data revealed, the system of control, nevertheless, could not provide the level of required quality, because the 
traditional consciousness within the workshop prevailed. This consciousness meant meeting the plan by any means. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar It was clear that even the many layers of managers could not provide a system of effective control from the workplace to the whole plant. That 
is why changing the whole system of organisation of the production process and process of control was needed. How the situation changed will be clear from the next chapter.
\par \pard\plain \s1\sb480\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\fs36\lang2057\kerning28 \page {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267967}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554684}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378649}Chapter 2. The Doncaster Plant after Privatisation{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267967}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554684}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378649} 
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
As we have seen in the last chapter, the railways were traditionally very bureaucratic, surrounded by rules and regulations with a hierarchical management structure and a complex division of labour and responsibilities. This meant that there was a substan
tial role for informal relation
s at all levels. However, the growing pressure of competition put this structure under increasing pressure. The first attempts to change the system had very little impact because they came up against the structure of informal relations, leading to increas
ed conflict but no improvements in efficiency. It was only with the privatisation of the plant that the pressure for change became intense and it went through a much more radical restructuring. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar The plant was sold to ABB in June 1995. ABB is an International Company, which emerged in 1987 from two companies - \lquote Asea\rquote  a Swedish company, created in 1890 and \lquote Brown Boveri\rquote 
, which took shape in 1891. Perhaps, now it is one of the most competitive enterprises in Europe or even in the world. The main aim of the co
mpany is to organise a firm which would be competitive on the basis of combining the global scale, world-class technology and the local market. The main slogan of ABB\rquote s president Percy Barnevic is \lquote Think global, act local\rquote 
. With the creation of ABB the economic environment in Europe has considerable changed. Today ABB employs 240,000 people around the world. 
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 At the top of the company sits Percy Barnevic and 12 colleagues on the executive committee. The group, which meets every three weeks, is responsible for ABB\rquote 
s global strategy and performance.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Reporting to the executive committee are leaders of the 50 or so business area (BAs), located world-wide, into which the company\rquote 
s products and services are divided. The BAs are grouped into 8 business segments, for which different members of the executive committee are responsible.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Each BA has a leader responsible for optimising the business on a global basis. Alongside the BA structure sits a country structure.{\cs16\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s22\qj\sl480\slmult1
\widctlpar \f40\fs20\lang2057 {\cs16\super \chftn } W. Taylor, 1991, pp. 91-104.}}
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267968}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554685}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378650}The organising logic of ABB.{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267968}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554685}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378650}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The main principles of ABB are:
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar First of all the company is organising a {\ul global network}
. Executives around the world will make decisions in accordance with the global requirement for a particular product without regard for national borders. The second dimension is {\ul collecting traditionally organised national companies}
. And then trying to combine the two dimensions together. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar In Britain ABB acted as before: they bought similar plants in Crewe, Derby, and recently in Doncaster, and now that they had the whole set of the best maintenance plants, they began to reshape their organisations. 

\par \pard\plain \s2\sb360\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\i\f5\fs28\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267969}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554686}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378651}Reorganisation{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267969}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554686}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378651}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The main weaknesses of the company from the General Director\rquote s view, who was in charge from June 1995, just after privatisation, to 9 September 1996:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 I think we recognised that we are m
oving to a company, or we acquired a company that traditionally did not have to compete for its work. It was traditional that it was a government owned company, because there was X amount of work to be done for British Railways, it was called out.. 
\lquote We will give you that, we will give you that..., we will give you that..., we will give you that...\rquote .  It was called out to all different works. And they didn\rquote t compete for it.{\ul 
 So there was not a competitive element within the culture of the workforce and deeper in the culture of the organisation.}
 It was not organised in order to compete. So we also were aware that disappointments were associated with materials management, and a system to support that did not allow them to become a modern company. Because basically the buying system was basically 
a procurement system, and it was based on history, on what they used last year. What we need in this business is a system which looks forward - there is a system which is called an FRP system (Factory Resource Planning). A system {\ul 
which can forecast what you are going to need, a system which looks forward}. We also knew that the culture of the people was in terms{\ul  of the level of overtime,}
 assessable within British Rail. Basically they manipulated all the systems to give themselves the highest rate that was possible{\ul  like fourteen shifts overtime. Personal allowances}
. Allowances to do everything, dirty work allowance, spectacles, protective clothes, overalls. Because we\rquote ve been here before, I worked for BREL in Crewe before it 
was privatised in 1988. ABB came along in 1991 to Crewe. I was one of the old culture. I worked for the nationalised British Rail workshops. British Rail Engineering Limited. So, I\rquote 
ve seen the other side of it. And when ABB came, they really fit the business. In the same way, now we came here and adopted the same approach.
\par \pard\plain \qj\li180\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 What ABB did in the first place was to appoint their own people to the most important positions within the workshop:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 And what we did, into that site we brought 10 ABB people, we brought m
yself as a General Manager, people to manage materials. We brought commercial people in. And to make it more competitive, to become more customer focused, we also brought people in to manufacturing. It is strategic points. There is another way of doing th
at which is to take people out from this organisation and put them to other ABB sites, where they will be surrounded by people, thinking in a different way. And leave them for six months, and then take them back out and put them back to work as managers. 
But before that we do cross fertilisation.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Another reorganisation was concerned with the whole organisational structure. The whole organisational hierarchy was reorganised with many fewer layers and made much more clear:
\par \pard \qc\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar General manager
\par I
\par Product manager
\par I
\par Team leader
\par I
\par Team
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 It was traditionalist people who set up the organisation as a hierarchical organisation. Lots and lots of layers of managers. Nevertheless we stopped all this. We\rquote 
ve got to cut it right down. We\lquote ve got to take out layers of management, we\rquote ve got to pass the responsibility down, and we\rquote ve got to make people accountable on the lowest level. To make them accountable and responsible. We\rquote 
ve got to give them the authority. We are going to trust them. And somebody would find it difficult to do t
hat, because we have to leave people to get on with it, you should do a lot of work before people would be able to do that. This is why the empowerment status came into focus. And why we did that was that focus on {\ul core business}
. Say this is your Doncaster business is, then break it down into small units, product groups. 
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
Things that logically sit together. They might sit together for many reasons - it might be the same customer, similar type of work, growth products in one area. This is how we organise our product group management. And then we make those units accountable
 in isolation, one manager with the small team. Although we get clear visibility on the results of that small unit, then we put all the results back together at the centre and send them off. 
But what is important is that we get visibility of the job that is being done. Visibility is growing up. The only way to do that  is to focus on core business, break  those products down into groups, manageable groups, put them all accountable, make them 
accountable, take away all the excuses, get them to deliver. And it is not calm, but if you find actually the right people, they will  be able do it. It is not difficult. They can calculate their expenses at home. Here it is the same thing\'85 
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar About the workers. When they arrive at the factory, they leave their brains at the gates. {\ul Poor management traditionally were not be able to use their brain}
. We get them to use it, we get them into a team, into small groups, get them involved, give them some pride and respect that my team is performing well. I am on top of the league (Interview with the General Manager of the plant, September 1996).
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 According to the new reorganisation, the Team Leaders would play the key role. The General Manager thought that the main qualities of the Team Leaders should be {\i potential - not power.}
 The General Manager, according to his words,{\i  }
was collecting all the material about new management techniques all around the world. He tried to share his philosophy with the employees, which is why he constructed all material and presented it on the boards throughout the company. Here we can find som
e of his sentences of wisdom:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 As teamwork becomes a fact of corporate life, managers must become facilitators rather than commanders.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Give a man a fish you feed him for a day. Teach a man to fish, you feed him for life.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
The confusion over what supervisors should be called is a reflection of the way their roles are changing. But, whether you call them team leaders, cell leaders, section leaders, foremen or supervisors, these first line managers are key people, and need to
 be developed as such.
\par \pard \s18\qr\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Works management, August 1993. p.27
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar First line supervisors make it happen. The success of any organisational change depends on their ability to inspire the commitment 
and enthusiasm of their teams. Without that, the change will almost certainly fail.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 How then can an organisation turn its supervisors into leaders?
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 1.The first step is to appoint people with leadership potential.. .. They need good interpersonal, listening and communications skills
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
2. A self-directed, empowered team is a group of employees who are responsible for a whole work process, delivering product or service to a customer, whether internal or external. Team members work together to improve their opera
tion, handle day-to-day problems and plan and control their work. 
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 While I asked the General Manager his view on Team Leaders, and their communication with the team, he presented me with his views about the direction in which the organisation should move:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 The Team Leader\rquote 
s job is probably one of the toughest. At some point in time we are looking for what we call total harmonisation of terms and conditions. When there is no such thing as a man on the shop floor, who is on wages, and the man in the office, wh
o is on salary. We are looking for an employee, who operates on the same terms and conditions. And the only thing different is with the rate of pay, of a reward corresponding to their responsibility. Anything else would be equal. And I think before we get
 to that situation it will take some years, before white collar and blue collar see themselves as one. The system in the UK is now with the cross-status. We are breaking our past and that is a Japanese approach.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Coming back to your question about Team Leade
rs. Until you get total harmonisation and the acceptance that we are all equal. Someone, somewhere should break that gap between the manual worker and the office worker, and that perception about white collar worker and blue collar worker, and that applie
s to the Team Leader. Now looking to all those levels, including that level of first line supervisor, I still believe that was the key and we appear to support that level. And we need all we can to do whatever we can to support him to get the results to t
hose people. Because at the end of the day that is the one who is truly undervalued. And unless we are supporting them in their objectives, then we do not want them undervalued. That\rquote 
s why we are here. So a smart TL has to work hard and it is like fox hunting. He has to set up both sides of events. Because he has assumed to be - he has to support the lads, but at the same time he has management responsibility. It is a hard job. 

\par \pard\plain \qj\fi-180\li180\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Where did you find supervisors?
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 All the TLs  were chosen from within the plant. F
irst of all it was the people from the shopfloor. Existing chargehands and existing supervisors. We put them through the series of interviews and a psychometric tests, to make sure that people would suit this position. But we also have seen how they perfo
rmed. We appointed them last week and we are providing several types of training on things like time attendance control, absence control, and so on. Some of them have been on managing change programs - it is an excellent course provided by internal people
. ABB PROCOM - ABB training program.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar We found it is very useful to use these people, they\rquote ve been there. They know our culture. They know the business side. And if you get outsiders, they lost the process, they don\rquote 
t have contacts.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Thus, the new management team reorganised the site according to their new policy. The management structure was also reorganised:
\par \pard \qc\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\pvpara\posy0\dxfrtext180\dfrmtxtx180\dfrmtxty0 {\b New management structure September 1996
