\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx1\dppty289\dpx7421\dpy298\dpxsize1\dpysize289\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20}}{\b 
\par }{\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8307\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx1\dppty289\dpx6701\dpy245\dpxsize1\dpysize289\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20}{\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8291\dpline\dpptx721\dppty0\dpptx0\dppty289
\dpx6701\dpy101\dpxsize721\dpysize289\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20}}{\b 
\par }\pard \qj\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\box\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \tx5310\pvpara\posy0\dxfrtext180\dfrmtxtx180\dfrmtxty0 \tab \tab \tab Entrance {\b 
\par }\pard \qj\sa120\widctlpar\box\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp20 \pvpara\posy0\dxfrtext180\dfrmtxtx180\dfrmtxty0 {\b \line 
\par }\pard \qj\sa120\widctlpar {\b 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267970}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554687}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378652}Reorganisation of the production process{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267970}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554687}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378652}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 {\b I.}
 The main reorganisation was concerned with the reorganisation of the production process: the plant was moved to one product operations. It was meant to find out the most profitable services in the lo
cal market and become the best producer of these services. At the beginning there were 5 product groups on the site: 
\par \pard \qj\li720\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 1. Repairable support product group.
\par 2. Vehicle repair product group.
\par 3. Bogies product group.
\par 4. Locomotives/ multiple units product group.
\par \pard \qj\li720\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 5. Special projects.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar The main emphasis was on Quality/Cost. The main focus on fast turnaround. The management agreed with the customers about 7 days operations: it means that all repairs should be done within 7 days. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Then, DMU was moved to Crewe with all its equipment. This meant another reorganisation and by September 1996 only 4 product groups were left.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\b II}
. Another major reorganisation was concerned with flexible team working. Flexibility was strictly emphasised. Flexible team working meant less and less demarcations across trades. The fitter would do the electrician\rquote 
s work. 90 % of the labourers had gone, but the shopfloor was kept clean by the workers in teams.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\b III.} If before there were calculations based on historic records - they kept materials an
d spares for the output equal to the number of vehicles the plant did in the previous months, so that the plant kept stock for 6 months ahead. Now, in the changing environment in that period, business might be cancelled. So the plant can\rquote 
t afford to buy too much material in advance. The only thing they can do is to hold a week\rquote s supply of materials. The reorganisation of the system of storage allowed the plant to reduce overheads from 2.4 million to 1.5 million pounds.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\b \page Materials statistics (pounds):
\par }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft90 \cellx2745\cellx5119\cellx6840\cellx8280 \pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl Aug 95{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl Aug 96{\f133 \cell }\pard 
\s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl % Change{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft90 \cellx2745\cellx5119\cellx6840\cellx8280 \pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 STOCK{\f133 \cell }\pard 
\s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 3,005,941 {\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 2,113,626{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl - 30%{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 
\f40\lang2057 STOCKHEADS{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 8,314{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 4,819{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl - 42%{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\pard\plain 
\s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 KITTED ITEMS{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl (903){\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl (2300){\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl +154%{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 
{\f133 \row }\pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 TWO BIN ITEMS{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl (NIL){\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl (686){\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl N/A{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain 
\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 
\par SUPPLIERS:{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl {\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl {\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl {\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 
\f40\lang2057 TOTAL SPEND
\par previous 12 months{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 25,240,000 {\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 27,065,000{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl +7,23%{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row 
}\pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 (RAILPART){\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 21,091,054 {\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 22,724,000{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl + 7,75%{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain 
\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 TOTAL NUMBER{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 2,408{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 400{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl - 83%{
\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft90 \cellx2745\cellx5119\cellx6840\cellx8280 \pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 WITH COMMITMENT{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 840{
\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 228{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl - 72%\cell \pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 \row \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Apart from this, a contract with an external firm for the delivery of small bolts and screws was organised. As a consequence of this contract a {\i\ul two bin system} was organised as well T
he two bin system provided each week all the sorts of small bolts and screws, which the plant needed for its effective production.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\b IV}. In the workshop itself the \lquote under one roof concept\rquote  was implemented which meant all managerial staff in one office near the main production.
\par {\b V.} Within the workshop the level of overtime was reduced considerably. The use of overtime in the new circumstances did not exceed 8% per month.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Soon after privatisation the new administration decided to make redundancy of the staff. The main aim of it was that the plant should be competitive in the new market environment. In order to be competitive -{\ul 
 it should work with fewer people, but more efficiently.}
 There were two sets of redundancy. The first redundancy was on a voluntary basis, the second one - half voluntary - half compulsory. Altogether the manpower reduction was from 627 in June 1995 to 312 by September 1996.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar The main feature of the two redundancies was that people who left the plant were selected on the seniority basis - \lquote first in - last out\rquote 
. Because of that principle, the younger people left the plant. The older ones remained in the plant. The selection procedure caused some problems on the shop floor because the youngest people left, who can be flexible, open minded. Withi
n the plant mainly people were left, who had worked on the plant for an average of 20 - 30 years. It is obvious that they faced more difficulties, because they were not so flexible as the younger generation. They were one of the obstacles to implementing 
flexibility on the shop floor. If younger people quite easily accepted the changes:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 I don\rquote 
t mind flexibility - you are expected to do any jobs. Before - it was a strict demarcation line. Now - no (Interview with a young worker from the coaching bay, who worked on the plant for 2 years, September 1996).
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
The representatives of older generations were very upset about this. It was very difficult for them to change their mind. Because before they had allowances for doing the job for another trade. Now they should do it for the same money:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 
We are faced with redundancies. They want us to be more adaptable and more flexible. Now electricians are sweeping up. Before, we did overtime to go with it (Interview with a former trade union leader, 57-old worker on the loco 51, September, 1996).

\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Here is a view of one of the Team Leaders:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 They give me respect. And all that. And if you ask them to do some work you can get people who don\rquote 
t like being told: when you been working on the shop floor for a long time and some of them are older then me and they don\rquote t like being told, \lquote You do this\rquote , they just don\rquote t like it. Say, you\rquote 
ve got the old brigades, who have been here for 25-30 years and who will not accept the change. ABB with their policy of flexibility they want you to do a bit
 of everything if you can to make the job proper and to make a profit (Interview with a Team Leader, September 1996).
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The other question comes up with redundancy, whether the workshop became more efficient or not? In order to answer this question we will try to reveal people\rquote 
s opinion. But it is clear that there are a lot of contradictions on the shop floor:
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
From the one side, there was a lot of evidence on the shop floor that the situation with the organisation of the production process improved considerably. It is much more organised now. The two bin system was implemented, material storage and a computing 
system organised. As one of the Team Leaders, who had worked on the plant since 1969 pointed out:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 Now - it is more production line. Before - it used to be a mess. No date for vehicles to go out. When it is ready, it goes out. Now - if it is not done - you\rquote 
ll be sacked. I think it works. I can see. Before - there was too much tea drinking. Too much time to get a job done. Now it is better.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The other worker from the Loco 91, class 37, 58 supported his opinion:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 
It is more organised now. Before - it was a mess, because when the vehicle came from the client it was dismantled and then repaired in different parts of the plant. Very often different parts were not r
epaired simultaneously. Now - it is more like a production line. Exchange of components.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 You can see organisation of things everywhere in the workshop, like for example with the material - there are special labels for the storage of material:
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \page Depot storage material coding plan:
\par \pard \qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8228\dpellipse\dpx-67\dpy149\dpxsize433\dpysize433\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr192\dpfillbgcg192\dpfillbgcb192\dpfillpat1}}
\par \pard \qj\fi720\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar ready to use
\par \pard \qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8197\dpellipse\dpx-67\dpy154\dpxsize433\dpysize433\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr223\dpfillbgcg223\dpfillbgcb223\dpfillpat1}}
\par \pard \qj\fi720\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar material for repair
\par 
\par \pard \qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8208\dpellipse\dpx-72\dpy-144\dpxsize433\dpysize433\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew20
\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr0\dpfillbgcg0\dpfillbgcb0\dpfillpat1}}\tab quarantined material
