\par }\pard \qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\lang1024 {\*\do\dobxcolumn\dobypara\dodhgt8198\dptxbx\dptxbxmar0{\dptxbxtext \pard\plain \qc\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 {\b\f5\fs32 
\par Think Tidy 
\par STEVE
\par Remember this is your home
\par 5 days a week
\par }}\dpx1728\dpy188\dpxsize5185\dpysize2304\dplinesolid\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0\dplinew40\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr255\dpfillbgcg255\dpfillbgcb255\dpfillpat1}}{\fs28 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }\pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar From the other side it is necessary to say there were very contradictory things on the shop floor. And the question to be asked is, {\ul apart from the new organisational changes, was there any kind of investment?}
 There was no any evidence of any kind of new technology at all. On the contrary - a lot of work was transferred to other plants. Most of the equipment was dismantled and taken out. Here is a bitter view of one of the workers, who had worked almost thirty
 years at the plant:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 This restructuring, which they implemented - is very questionable. They\rquote ve taken away our work. Lathes, equipment. Now we are less able to do any work. 
\par The factory now is very unsettled. New confidence - declining workload. New management - new conditions. 
\par The only view we have of the new managers is distrusting. The General Manager is like a robber. He was a Crewe man - he came here and robbed all our equipment. They were stealing our work:
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar - brascover (valves)
\par - power units
\par - traction motors...
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar The General Manager - he is very polite, he would listen. But it is in theory. In reality - 16 months he was here - and took away our work.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar The main General Manager\rquote s idea was to spend nothing on restructuring. 
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Traditionally - there were 4 bays. They altered the cranes. The disruption in the works is unbelievable...
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 From the one hand all the upper managers were talking about the elimination of wasted time, and they\rquote 
ve done a lot in order to cancel it. On the other hand - there is a lot of evidence that people have got more spare time then before:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 I have more spare time then ever to do work for myself - to do foreigners and something else. Th
e only problem now - it is material. If before we had a lot of it laying on the floor - now you can\rquote t get it.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
With the reduction of the workload and new agreements with the customers all the vehicles now arrive according to a new schedule: Coming on Monday - off on Wednesday. Coming on Wednesday - off on Friday. 
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 Now very often, the vehicle doesn\rquote t come in on Wednesday, so we have no workload for the rest of the week.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar - What are you doing then?
\par Sweeping the floor, tossing it off.
\par \lquote Monday morning - not very much to do\rquote .
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The situation that there is nothing to do is proved in a diary of one of the workers on the shop floor:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 {\b\i Friday, 20/09/96.
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar No work.
\par Clean up. 
\par Wash windows. 
\par What next.?
\par ABB. We care about you.!.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Moreover, after the previous redundancy within the plant there is a lack of people with particular skills. That is why the managers are seeking anybody who is able to fulfil some jobs:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 {\b\i Monday. 16/09/96
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Finished testing.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 6 resets reservoirs required in other shop for morning shift. Nobody to take them So I take them myself on forklift. I have never driven forklift before: FUN!!!
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\b\i Thursday 19/09/96
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Team leader comes up with another job. I am needed to repair air/water separator/regulator valve. Again I am the only person on the works who knows how to do them. I do it. 
\par No problem!\rquote {\cs16\i\super  (}Extract from a diary of the working week, written by a fitter in the plant. September 1996.)
\par \pard\plain \s2\sb360\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\i\f5\fs28\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267971}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554688}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378653}Relations with the customers after privatisation{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267971}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554688}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378653}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 According to the new reorganisation of the production process there was a move towards \lquote 7 day operations\rquote 
. It was agreed with the customers that all the vehicles would be delivered on time. If there will be a delay in delivery - the plant should pay {\ul 500 pounds per day per vehicle}
 to the customers The results of such a policy is shown in the following chart: 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar CLASS 91 OUTPUT FROM 02/04/96 TO 26/07/96
\par \trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \cellx2340\cellx3676\cellx5012\cellx6348\cellx7684\cellx9020 \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\intbl Locomotive \cell \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\intbl 91020 \cell 91003 \cell 91002 \cell 91016 \cell 91026
\cell \pard \widctlpar\intbl \row \trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \cellx2340\cellx3676\cellx5012\cellx6348\cellx7684\cellx9020 \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\intbl Actual days \cell \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\intbl 16 \cell 15 \cell 15 
\cell 15 \cell 18\cell \pard \widctlpar\intbl \row \trowd \trgaph108\trleft-108 \cellx2340\cellx3676\cellx5012\cellx6348\cellx7684\cellx9020 \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\intbl Contract days \cell \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\intbl 
15 \cell 15 \cell 15 \cell 15 \cell 15\cell \pard \widctlpar\intbl \row \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
\par 91020 - 1 additional day for all circulation traction
\par 91028 - 1 additional day for oil circulation traction, plus 2 days work on after trial snags.
\par The General Managers\rquote  view on the relations with the customers was as follows:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 Relations with the customers have become much more{\ul  formal now}. Here they had cosy relationships before, which was okay. They were good relationships, but they\rquote 
ve got to be on a formal basis. For clarification. You tell how people are coming in saying, or just picking up the phone and saying: \lquote Oh, by the way, I don\rquote t want that doing, I want it to come here\rquote . {\ul It has to be formalised}
, contract reviewed. If we look at penalties for days late - at various level - it is quite severe. We get penalised 500 pounds per day, and without all formal notifications, when you start getting to the commercial aspects, then you really got problems, 
if you got nothing in writing. So, it has to be formal, that\rquote s not intended to be obstructive to the customer, it\rquote s intended to ensure that there will be no problems.
\par \pard\plain \qj\li180\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 \endash  So do you think that before, it was much more informal?
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 Yes, less professional. You\rquote 
ve got to be professional. The customer is doing a lot of things with professionals. I think that you could take the view that the customer might see you as being inflexible, if you are not prepared to keep changing all these things around. 
But at the end of the day I think he will respect the fact that you are professional. That\rquote s important.
\par I strongly believe that at the end of the day he respects that I take the formal position. That is our position now. It doesn\rquote t mean that we are irresponsible people and we would say \lquote Yes\rquote  to everything. And then having said \lquote 
Yes\rquote , we find out that we can\rquote t deliver. So that can lead to the customer perception going down. It is so easy to say \lquote Yes\rquote , and then you fail. I think its true.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The head of one of the product groups:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 
Customer needs - far better now. When we first moved to the multiple units - it was embarrassing to talk - too much was left without notice. But now we are more satisfied now than before.
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267972}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554689}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378654}Quality problems{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267972}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554689}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378654} 

\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 As was mentioned before - the changing of attitudes towards quality was a very long procedure. The reorganisation within the plant touched many things, but it was impossible to change people
\rquote s minds so quickly. The problems of quality still remained within the plant. That is why there was a constant struggle for better quality. 
\par \pard\plain \s18\qc\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 {\b\i AdTranz
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\b Quality
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\b\i Policy Statement
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar It is a policy of ABB Daimler-Benz transportation (Customer Support) Ltd to totally satisfy the needs of its customers and to dedicate all operations towards maintaining its positi
on as a world class company.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar This will be achieved through constantly examining the company\rquote s performance and pursuing the need for continuous improvement.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar By investing in people the company will apply the philosophy of Total Quality through:
\par \pard \s18\qj\li1440\sb60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar - the commitment of all its employees;
\par - fully understanding the customer\rquote s requirements;
\par - a willingness to combat waste and reduce costs;
\par \pard \s18\qj\li1440\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar - seeking out constant improvements;
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar The achievement of these quality goals will be determined by the dedication shown in our work.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar All our commitment and actions must be recognised as an expression of quality of service and product.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar ABB Daimler-Benz Transportation (Customer Support) Ltd\rquote 
s quality systems conform to the international standard ISO 90000 and provide the framework for achieving high quality products and services.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar The quality system is all described in division quality manuals and lower tier working procedures.
\par \pard \s18\qr\li245\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Steve Svard
\par Managing Director
\par February 1996
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Describing the system with quality, it is worth mentioning the whol
e system of connections between the plant and the customers, because now it has become much more complicated. When British Rail was privatised, the master plan was that four owners would have the whole of the rolling stock and all vehicles. They let them 
out to a limited number of leasing companies, which run the trains. They would run trains according to specific rules. It was going to be broken down into hundreds of companies. What happened in fact was that several large companies rent the vehicles. The
 o
wners of the companies are required by law to send the vehicles for repair after a certain number of miles. To carry out a larger repair, they need some plants such as Doncaster which have the facilities for lifting the vehicles off their bogies and there
 can also be technical qualifications. The company that rents the vehicle is under contract to do minor repairs : fuel injector changes, light changes - smaller repairs:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 When the vehicle is dispatched from Doncaster, we have to carry out all repairs require
d by the owner which are sometimes not as comprehensive as are required by the customer that is renting the vehicle. The owner could ask for a bogie change, the customer wants, when the bogie has been changed, everything else should be working. We inform 
the person that is renting the vehicle, the Depot. Just one silly example. When the head wire is not working, the vehicle could only run in daytime safely. So we can only dispatch the vehicle on Rail Track property with the condition that it only runs in 
daylight. The person renting the vehicle, would say - repair the head wire, but he doesn\rquote 
t have to pay for it. So we should go back to the person, who pays for the repair - the owner, asking him if he will pay for the head wire, and he has got two options: 
he could pay us, or he could insist that the vehicle goes back to the depot, because the head wire repair is under his agreement with a depot who is renting. So if you like you\rquote 
ve got a slight conflict of interest. We have to deal friendly with all three pa
rties. Obviously any one of them could disrupt our workload in all ways possible. Rail Track insist that when a vehicle has a large repair on the property of AdTranz - before it goes back into service - it must meet all the safety requirements, when it ca
me out of service. To comply with this, they insist on a rigorous safety inspection. On a qualified examiner, that\rquote 
s worked on the vehicle at least 5 years, make sure that we meet all safety standards, all work safety systems worked. (Extract from interview with a clerk, September 1996).
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
Having problems with quality repair, the leasing companies, who are renting the vehicles from the owner decided to put a special expert on the plant, who will check all the weak points within the plant. Here is an extract from my interview with such perso
n:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 
So what I am doing now for the Angel train leasing company is monitoring that overhaul is taking place in here now. They have a problem with quality. There were components from the depots that are from train operating unit
s and when they were coming back from overhaul, the quality was very poor. 
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar -Why?(M.K.)
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Various things they would get problems with units, with the quality of the paint on the bogies and they would complain about, because bearing in mind, when the unit comes here for overhaul, it\rquote 
s got to go out and operate something like three years before it comes back for another overhaul and obviously, when you are doing something you\rquote ve got to make sure that it will last that period. So the quality of overhaul isn\rquote t up
 to standard and things are going to deteriorate fairly quickly, and they are not going to get that life out of the vehicle or that components that changed. So what\rquote 
s happening at the moment is that one of the depots would complain about the quality, and the {\ul standard of workmanship from here}, and I was brought in to bring that {\ul quality standard up}. Because I know the vehicles, I\rquote 
ve worked on them I know the areas, where some people {\ul might take short cuts}, to do the job, where they shouldn\rquote t, and I am here to put my finger on it. To say : {\ul \lquote One minute, you are not doing it right, let\rquote s have it right
\rquote .} Once I\rquote 
ve been here for the last three months the quality has gone up and improved. But unfortunately in improving the quality, the cost increased slightly for the overhaul. What happened is - certain items and certain areas have caused us a problem - if
 you take a component an example like a complete bogie - on a sprinter, when it comes back in after three years of hard work it\rquote s covered in rust, dust. It gets very
 very dirty. Compacted and difficult to remove. Now. When they come in for overhaul. The first thing they have to do with bogies - they have to clean it. It\rquote 
s cleaned in a special cleaning machine with caustic, soda, hard biting chemical wash, which takes off most of the dirt. But unfortunately, it doesn\rquote 
t take it all off. So the implications of that is now, in order to get all things off, they have to put it through the cleaner twice. So the cost of it now is twice what it should be. It is the plant\rquote s resp
onsibility to clean it. Because the maintenance overhaul schedule says that you will take the bogie, clean it and overhaul it to the standard. Then obviously, when it\rquote s coming back they are painting over dirt, because they\rquote 
ve been cleaned that dirt may be there, for a while, might have been loosened. So that consequently, when it goes back to the depot, it may fall off. So the paint doesn\rquote t stay properly, so consequently you\rquote 
ve got problems with corrosion. Because the operating environment of bogies is very
 unfriendly. It gets everything thrown up on it from track dust, silicon based, you get rain, water, even the toilet defluent. And it gets all to the bogie, and it gets absolutely faulty. So in order to bring it back to a standard if you like to start aga
in, to make it last another three a half years, it\rquote 
s got to be on a top quality. So that was one of the complaints that came from the depots that the paint quality of the bogies was very very poor. Then we looked at it, we said yes, we agree with you, it is
 absolutely true. They are not cleaning these bogies properly and they are not painting them properly. So:
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar A). They aren\rquote t going to last the full three and half years and,
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar B). The people who own the vehicle and are paying for an overhaul aren\rquote t get a quality job, they\rquote ve been charged for it, they are not getting their money\rquote s worth. 
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar So at that end I am here, and I am saying to the works: \lquote Come on, do the job properly.\rquote 
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267973}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554690}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378655}Safety culture{\i {\*\bkmkend _Toc404267973}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554690}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378655}
\par }\pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 As well as the problems with quality, there are the same {\ul problems with safety}
. For all the years that production was the main dictator on the shop floor, people were not conscious about safety. And now everything is turned around. The world has changed and the safety values in it have become more important. As an engineer at the p
lant pointed out:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 So everybody is now disappearing running and running in circles, thinking safety. I don\rquote 
t want to get the blame for doing anything wrong. Because now in the privatised industry, everybody has got to be very very careful what they do.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 But the {\ul safety culture}
 also needs to be implemented for a long time in order to be an intrinsic part of the production process. Things could not happen overnight. That is why the person who is responsible for the safety at the plant has to struggle with the old approach:

\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 What\rquote s happened, I am now trying to build {\ul safety culture} within everybody. I\rquote 
ve got to make them aware of their duty to each other, person to person. It is not necessarily of top management saying - do this and do this, it is even down to the individuals.{\ul  That\rquote s the hardest part to install a safety culture,} {\ul 
when productivity is required.} Because what we have to do at the end of the day - is to make money. And my concern is - how they make money. These got mistaken way of safety.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Just as it was before privatisation with implementing Total Quality Management, there {\ul is a real gap in understanding of the safety culture} between the {\i top managers}
, who are really safety oriented and the {\i middle managers} on the shop floor. For many years they were kept 
in their positions because of their abilities to fulfil the plan. Among the new Team Leaders there are may be 30 per cent of people, who had a real appreciation of safety procedure within the plant. The rest of them didn\rquote t take it seriously:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 M.K.- To what extent is the management concerned about health and safety?
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Risk Manager- Top management - yes. But at the end of the day it is going to be down to the Team Leaders, young managers, they will see it as being involved to make money. {\ul I feel that if I was not
 here controlling them and advising them on  the health and safety regulations and legislation, which is place to contract, if we don\rquote 
t stick to that, we are going to be in deep trouble, i.e. will be prosecuted, it could close the company down for bad practices.} That is at the back of everybody\rquote 
s mind. The employees definitely know about, not legislation, but they know about when they are doing something wrong. They appreciate that. 
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar M.K.- But still doing it?
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar R.M.-Not necessarily. Some people won\rquote t. Others will. As you can appreciate it, looking on the sociological aspects of it - you can press on people to do things that they don\rquote 
t want to do. In some cases that may happen. That\rquote s why I come in and the safety guard with the safety. If they feel that the\rquote ve been pressurised into doing something that they shouldn\rquote 
t do. The first thing they can do - come to me. Consult with safety problems. And I will get them all the way if I consider that it\rquote s a hazard or dangerous activity. If they don\rquote t get satisfaction wit
h that. They obviously can go to unions, to the safety committee. And hopefully eliminate the hazardous activity. That\rquote 
s why we have the safety committee, which meets every month. And they talk to management, and I am concerned with safety procedures. Now we\rquote 
ve got a form -it is a full register of all our health and safety procedures, which were ex-BR. Now with AdTranz they are actual procedures. We are presenting procedures to managers, production unions, TL and they don\rquote t know about this. Or that
\rquote s the impression I get. They should have all this paper. Nothing has changed. We have not told them to remove the documents whatsoever.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\ul There are some regulations, and it could be a pressure of production - and they will try to avoid these regulations.} Like they say to you:{\ul  \lquote 
We are here to make money, not to monitor couple of it.\rquote } But if they don\rquote t look after the workforce, compensation claims would come from out of its profits. It\rquote 
s false economy. And lost time accidents. If a person is off work through an injury off work - then it is costing us money being off work and it can\rquote t produce, but it\rquote 
s entitled to a compensation claim against the company for not owing a duty of care, not supplying with an ear defender. Looses the eye  (Interview with the Risk Manager, September 1996).
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
This culture is very difficult to overcome. People are still thinking in the old manner. They are aware about other things, but because they did it for the whole of their lives, they continue to do it. The reasons could be different, but one
 of the reason again is the same, like in old times - the dictatorship of production:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 
Recently we had an accident at Derby, where the guy fell from a height of 30 ft, and was killed. But in here the guys work at the top of the vehicles with no straps on. And they know that.{\ul  Nothing changed.}
 They know the  working height - that is damned, and I can show you the old procedure. They know from the past - which was BR that this procedure exists.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar M.K.- In comparison with the situation in previous years. Do you consider that the situation has improved?
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\ul R.M. - It\rquote s improved from the production side - it\rquote s become tighter.} When it comes down to managerial attitudes - that had become lax - {\ul 
because of pulling the vehicles through the door}
 - to make an impression on the new buyers of the company. I know for a fact on one instance, with one vehicle - when something was being lifted with the old crane - the two blocks that lift the main hook, climbing to get there - a 6 inch piece shot out. 
Nobody was injured, but because the two blocks have gone through the upper limit switch, the crane was faulty, it should have switched off. An incident record was filled in. I said \lquote 
Right, that crane is totally out of action. I want a full investigation - tell me why it went through the upper limit switches, what caused it. What it needs to put it right. That crane is dead as far as I am concerned. That I\rquote 
ve done on Thursday or Friday that week. And I asked the maintenance team to do me an investigation. It was a part of their fault. {\ul The t
eam coming on the Saturday to do the work, and this is why they were coming from - five hundred pound a day down to contract, the production pressure on the managers meant that if they didn\rquote 
t have that crane, it was going to cost us 5 hundred pounds for that vehicle being out of standard.}
 If the HSE (Health and Safety Executive) had come in, nobody would have been able to use that, because it was on the investigation. They keep that out of action. These guys (maintenance team) were told to stand on top of t
he crane and tell him how high it can lift. So it\rquote s defeating the safety device. I was off, because I don\rquote t work Saturday.{\ul  They disregarded the health and safety procedures.} That\rquote s the concern I\rquote 
ve got. I since had the person in control of that situation sign a document saying that {\ul 
he accepts full responsibility and reliability for any actions or initiatives. And he signed it. The man  who was in control. And if anybody had an accident, injury, compensation claim, the company would} {\ul not be liable, it would b
e the actual individual, who is  telling him or asking people going there and doing things. The procedures are there}  (Interview with the Risk Manager, September 1996).{\ul 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267974}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554691}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378656}Shortage of material{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267974}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554691}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378656}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 As we can see from the above, the implementation of the {\ul \lquote two bins system\rquote } has eliminated the shortages of small screws, bolts and so on. As the workers pointed out:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 They\rquote ve been trying to incorporate the two bins system. It has been implemented about 5 months ago. If one is empty on the top - put the other one forward. A
nd the empty bin will get refilled in a three-four day period. That is filled by a private firm. It is working now and there are never shortages of screws. 
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Now we don\rquote t bother about cutting the long screws. We don\rquote t have that problem now. So now there are as many as you want of all different sizes what you want.
\par \pard\plain \qj\fi-180\li180\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 {\i  
\par }\pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar At the same time when I talked to the Team Leader, he explained to me that he has problems with material:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 {\i M.K. -If the vehicle is due on Wednesday, would it be possible for you to delay?
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\i T.L.- If there is a delay, it costs us 500 pounds a day. Liquidated damages. If we do not deliver the vehicle at a specific time, it would cost us 500 a day. We do it on time. We\rquote 
ve got material problems. I\rquote ve got material problems. The material should have been ordered earlier. 
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\i M.K.- What kind of material? 
\par T.L. It\rquote s a metal strip. We have to use them.
\par M.K -How will you manage this problem? You will order this material?
\par T.L- We have known about this problem at least for 6 weeks, but still nothing has been sorted out.
\par M.K-Why?
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\i T.L- It is not me, it is the office. If they sent the order for materials to the National stores, it would be correct, but they didn\rquote t. So they have been sending it only now. 
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\i M.K -How you will resolve this problem?}{\b\i 
\par }\pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\i T.L- When the office would manage it, while they would send us the material, we would manage it (}Interview with a Team Leader, September 1996).{\i 
\par }\pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The engineer explained to me the situation with the shortage of parts as well:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 M.K.-When I\rquote ve been here last time, there was a lot of shortage of parts. How has it changed now?
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar E. -It is still the same. We still have the shortage of components, yes. Again I am giving you an example. Because we\rquote 
ve got conventional DMU-s, which are still running out the system, some of those are now 35-40 years old. When they were built there was 
still plenty of supply of materials. They were built with the technology and expertise of that time. Now, because things have moved on, things been improved, a lot of components become obsolete. The original manufacturers don\rquote 
t want to make them any more, because it is costly for them to do that, and they want to do things for now, not for the past. At the moment exhausts, which create vacuum for the brakes, they are the only vacuum brake vehicles. Vacuum brakes are mo
re efficient then air brakes, so there is a serious shortage of exhausts for those vehicles, and we\rquote 
ve only got what are on them - only a few spares. And those few spares are rapidly disappearing. And there is going to come a time when we will not know what
 we are going to do to replace them. Either then we have to find a lot of components which will make vacuum, or we just have to get rid of the vehicles. Because unfortunately, what was happening - all the vehicles, which have been scrapped, their componen
ts have either been used up or lost. Now, those vehicles again, because we\rquote ve got nothing to replace them with, the leasing company haven\rquote 
t anything to replace them with, it is only again the problems we will get with an overhaul, which will probably start in July next year. I\rquote 
ve been asked by the leasing company, and this is Angel trains contracts, to look at what we call the critical list of spares, and I actually have drawn the list of it. I\rquote ve already started on it, and basically, that\rquote s :
\par \pard \s18\qj\li1440\sb60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar exhausts for brakes,
\par engine compressors,
\par wheel sets
\par final drives
\par engine suspensions
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar They\rquote ve got an automatic fire system, which was developed from aircraft. It\rquote s pretty well obsolete by now, and you can\rquote t get some pieces for it. What we\rquote 
ve got to do is to try to fit a modern automatic fire system on to them. Now, obviously, new components may or may not fit in that place. If they don\rquote 
t fit, we can probably look and make some drawings to make them fit. We are going to modify the vehicles. To take the equipment which is c
osting us money. So someone has got to take a decision, whether the cost to fit the new equipment is going to be worth it for the length of time the vehicle would be around. Or it would be better to scrap them now, to get rid of them, because the cost of 
mo
difying vehicles now to bring them up to a standard is pretty horrendous, so someone has got to take this decision. What I can do is just recommend, and I have some ideas to put forward to them, what to do to replace some of these components, but it is go
ing to cost a lot of money. So they need to make a decision now. Because those vehicles are now at the end of their working life. So they need to make a decision, is it worth to do that or to get rid of them. And build new.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar I also insist on the conditions t
hat bogies do not lay in the open air. Because now they are storing them in the open air. And we consulted the managers not to do it. And only now, because there is some spare place, they will put all the bogies in one area under the roof. 
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar M.K.- Cannibalisation - before it was. But now - because there are some experts, like you, here - they can\rquote t do it?
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar E.- No.
\par M.K.- And even if they will do it, you will see it.?
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar E.- On the exhausted things - I know that {\ul there aren\rquote t any available} that Railpart (former NSC - M.K.) don\rquote t have any that there just aren\rquote 
t any available anywhere. Now, last week, I rang to the vehicle dismantlers and I asked them if they had got any of these exhausts, and they rang me back and told me that they actually found two. They were amon
gst different pieces. So I said to him, put it to one side, I\rquote ll get somebody to buy them. Now they\rquote ve got to buy them. At one time they used to say \lquote come and take them\rquote . That was the informal side. Now we\rquote 
ve got to buy them. (So informal things are now also formalised - M.K.) Because we\rquote ve got a little expression of this.(He made some movement of fingers -to put money inside his hand - M.K.) I don\rquote t know if you\rquote 
ve seen this gesture before. It means that they need to put money in my hand. Yes.\rquote 
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267975}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554692}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378657}Control of work{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267975}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554692}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378657}

\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
Within the organisation there is a big move towards more organised work. It was clearly the intention of the upper managers that they would not bear the previous situation, when people were drinking tea or coffee not at a particular time. 
\par All the workers as before had to clock on and off, with minor differences - there is no time allowed to be late. Even that two minutes for being late, which was allowed before privatisation, was cancelled. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
The organisation of work has become tighter than ever before. The meal breaks, which were 20 minutes under BREL (which was not too much from any point of views) were reduced to 15 minutes per shift. Within the plant a paper about the meal breaks was circu
lated, originating from the top:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qr\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 Adtranz 17/9/96
\par MEAL BREAK TIMES
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar To clarify the situation:
\par Monday to Thursday -
\par \pard \s18\qj\li720\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 11. to 11.20 Mornings and Mid-Days
\par 17.30 to 17.50 Afternoons.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 5 minutes washing off time is included in the above, therefore team members are expected to be at their place of work immediately before and after the above times.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Friday:
\par \pard \s18\qj\li720\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar A refreshment break of 5 minutes will be taken by agreement with your Team Leader
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Saturday and Sunday:
\par \pard \s18\qj\li720\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 1/2 day - No meal or refreshment break will be authorised
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 So the policy is towards full use of each employee. But the implementation of this policy was far from perfection. Walking down the shop there were a lot of people wasting their time:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 Ian - standing on the third bay, doing nothing.
\par \endash  What are you waiting for?
\par I am waiting for my supervisor. When he comes back, he will give me a job.
\par \endash  For how long have you been waiting for him?
\par Not that long, may be fifteen minutes.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 What was interesting was that the wasting of time concerned not only the workers, but supervisors as well:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 When I asked one of the supervisors to speak to me, 
he told me, with a very serious expression on his face that he was busy. It was not a meal break, I had a chance to have a look on the table, and there were several coupons for the national football pools, which he was trying  to fill in. I could not hide
 my curiosity and asked him: what is it. He answered:
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar I put a lot of money there, so please, come in 15 minutes.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 According to the new terms and conditions, all the workers should sign for their work from September 1996. It was done in order to tighten the c
ontrol within the enterprise and in order to increase the level of responsibility.
\par At the moment I was at the plant  this rule had just been implemented and people were not in a hurry to sign for their own work. When I asked the Team Leader, why it is happening he said:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 They should fill them in, but not everybody does it. Team Leaders  still sign the full jobs. It is changing.
\par Before - it was a system of block cards, where there was the amount of work necessary to be fulfilled on a particular vehicle. Detailed description of kind of work for 3-4 days.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar According to the new system of terms and conditions all the tradesmen should do it themselves.
\par But they still put OK in front of all job description, for example:
\par - to change valve - OK
\par - electrical... - OK
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
When I spoke to their team leader, he told me that he has a whole book, where he writes down which shift is doing what. He told me that he will gather them together and educate them. If they accepted new terms and conditions \endash  they voted for it 
\endash  they should do it. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar So, the document control has become more formalised and been tightened.
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267976}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554693}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378658}Resistance to changes?{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267976}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554693}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378658}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
Unhappy with the effect of privatisation and with the increased intensity of working the workers (especially the old one) were willing to develop various forms of informal actions, and collective and individual resistance to change. But actually they had 
almost no chance to resist either overtly or covertly. To understand why workers were unable to mount effective resistance it is necessary to consider the view of the workers on this matter:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 \endash  Can you go slow or do some other industrial actions?
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar You wouldn\rquote t get involved in any industrial disputes. Nobody wants that. Most people\rquote s future here is a little grimy. You\rquote 
ve got to do the job and you just do it. (Worker - 17 years on the plant. Loco, DMU, wagon (RFS)).
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar I don\rquote 
t mind flexibility - you are expected to do any jobs. Before - it was a strict demarcation line. Now - no. We did resist - 8 years ago. The climate is changed now. New working conditions. If y
ou resist - you are out. Not too happy. Apart from doggy work.  We have to do our work. Basically I do what I am asked to. If you do try to escape then you\rquote ll get a reputation of{\ul  trouble maker}.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
The effect of privatisation was widely disliked. But among the workforce was an almost clear division between the old workers and the younger ones. The old workers thought that they worked hard enough for the whole of their life, and they deserved better 
conditions.
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 I gave everything to the enterprise. I should get as much as possible in return. I deserve it.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
The new organisation of work, including team working and multi-skilling, meant increasing of the intensity of their work. Naturally, when you have worked hard for twenty-thirty years, you want to relax at the end of the day. But in the Doncaster plant the
y had no chance for relaxation. In order to survive in the changing world, they\rquote ve got to be competitive, and for this reason they had to work twice as hard as in the past. Also I believe that for some reason, the
 propensity to flexibility is much more likely revealed among the younger ones. That\rquote 
s why the percentage of people who could change their mind was bigger among the youngest workers. They were more positive towards changes within their work and all the ne
w things which were required from them. Besides, they have a more pragmatic view towards the job. The old people consider work as the most valuable thing in their life. The younger people have another view:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 They think that we should devote ourselves to the 
full to this work. But this work is sometimes so boring that we prefer to get rid of it and have a rest. We are young and we would like to enjoy this life. Work is not the whole of life!
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Nevertheless, there were some minor forms of individual resistance. To begin with, some forms of \lquote sabotage\rquote 
 were reported. Although the managers and the workers reached an agreement about deleting the demarcation lines between the crafts, there were some kind of grey areas about the work of the labourers. The labourers cat2
 and cat1 historically cleaned the wagons and swept the floor. In new circumstances they were sent to clean the toilet, because there was not any other job at that moment for them to do. The labourers didn\rquote t agree with it. They didn\rquote 
t go to clean the toilet. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Also among the older workers the kind of go slow still was represented, when they didn\rquote 
t do work for a certain time. But among the oldest workers also there was some kind of apathy, because they thought that they would be sacked anyway. The cause of su
ch attitudes was in the character of the next redundancy. Managers announced that there will be another redundancy. But while the first two were on the basis of \lquote first in - last out\rquote 
 (according to the previous agreement with the workers), the following re
dundancy will be on the basis of several criteria, where the most valuable will be the level of flexibility, the amount of work done and level of skill. In these circumstances, the older workers understood that the main factor of their stability - the len
gth of service - had been removed. So they were in the most vulnerable position, and may be it was the main factor that they tried to resist the new things. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar J. Davidson in her analyses of resistance in a privatised industry stresses that 
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 
changes to the organisation of the labour process can (but do not always) provoke strong sentiments of opposition, but whether these are translated into action, and the form this action takes, and how successful this action is, all depend on a number of o
ther factors.{\cs16\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s22\qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs20\lang2057 {\cs16\super \chftn } O'Connell Davidson, 1994.}} 
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The
 most significant factor in these circumstances- is the existence and the strength of the Union. In the plant, where there were seven Trade Unions - all of them were very weak. But with the privatisation instead of unification and strengthening of their p
olicy, the Unions were very demoralised. Here is the view of one of the trade union leaders:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 Leaders of trade unions did not act well in those circumstances. Instead of unification - they were really aggressive to each other. Were protective about the trade
: that fitters better than electricians. There was a big disagreement within the trade union committee (Interview with trade union leaders. September 1996).
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Another factor which is worth mentioning here is the labour market conditions. Bray and Litter have observed that :
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 
in general, we can say that the labour market structure sets the agenda of the control relationship....  This is because the ultimate form of managerial control is the power to threaten loss of employment, a relation, which, in part, defin
es the nature of wage labour.{\cs16\i\super \chftn {\footnote \pard\plain \s22\qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs20\lang2057 {\cs16\super \chftn } Bray and Litter, 1988, p.569.}}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
But the labour market conditions reflect also the form and intensity of resistance. And interviews with people in the plant confirmed that conditions on the labour market in Doncaster and area was not very favourable in this sense:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 
I am now deeply concerned about redundancy, because there is no work to go to outside. It is frightening to see your colleagues, who left. 13 thousand people unemployed in Doncaster.
\par People who are younger - they could get a job. But mainly it is short term, low paid. No career, no structure. No future. 
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Many people who left were \lquote 50 +\rquote , so for them it was very difficult to get a job. Some of them got low paid job: near 3 pounds per hour. And we here have \'a3
6.32 p.h. But some of my colleagues have got no job at all (Extract from interview with a worker on the loco 37. September 1996).
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 It is clear that wider political and social features also influence resistance. And in these circumstances we can see that the workers understood that in the situation wh
ere the government tried to do everything in order to weaken the labour movement and be more than usually favourable to the employer - any kind of resistance will fail. And this feeling we can find in the answer of one of the workers to the question, why 
they can\rquote t resist:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 Now we are powerless - very demoralised workforce. Since I worked here - I\rquote 
ve seen everything. The miners were militant and they got bitten. Here, we are not militant. And also got bitten. So - nothing worked. What can you do? It is the government\rquote s policy (Extracts from interview with workers, \lquote Vagrem\rquote 
 plant, 1996).
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267977}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554694}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378659}The future - closing down?{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267977}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554694}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378659}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
What future is awaiting this company? It is very difficult to answer this question. When I was leaving the company, there was another set of redundancies ahead. People had very difficult feelings:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 Most people\rquote s future is a little grimy.
\par I\rquote ve been here 16 years, and I have never seen changes like these.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The majority of workers and managers did not believe in the future of the company:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 They are going to shut it any way.
\par I don\rquote t think there will be a future for me and for this company.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The most bitter feelings were concerned with the modern managers. Not very many people believed in the words of the managers. They judged them in accordance with their actions:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 They promote themselves as caring people. They talked about \lquote investment in people\rquote  and now these people are out of work.
\par They are still taking away our jobs. They are moving the equipment to Crewe. And Crewe is a Golden Child.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Privaty came here and we distrusted them. We don\rquote t believe them.
\par They gave us an ABB badge, paper bag. It is ridiculous.
\par There is a fear of the future. A great works with 150 years history has been squeezed.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
The most expected future is another set of redundancies. And maybe the works really will be turned into a profitable part of ABB, but it will be without those people who gave the plant all their life, all their strength, all their spirit and got nothing i
n return. And I want to finish the description of this plant with the words of one of the people who worked on the plant:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 
To be a public sector employee was satisfying to me. I have always taken an interest in society and social welfare. I am very concerned over the privatisation of the British railway network from both a personal and public interest. The whole affair is som
ething that I am neither able or willing to accept.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar What was regarded as a secure \lquote job for life\rquote , by choice, when I signed my apprenticeship indentures in 1969, no longer has the same meaning or assurance. Lo
ng service expectations and Company employees\rquote 
 rights, such as the BR pension fund, company redundancy terms, privileged travel cards, service seniority, and other conditions of employment earned over many years, and looked forward to, are now dishonourably threatened.{\cs16\i\super \chftn 
{\footnote \pard\plain \s22\qj\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs20\lang2057 {\cs16\super \chftn } Roger Holgate in Brown, 1995, pp. 57-58.}}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 In 1997 it will be 150 years since that time, when the land was purchased in Doncaster for constructing the Doncaster plant. After more then a century\rquote 
s great history of building and repairing vehicles, the plant\rquote s future is very questionable. What will happen? Time will show. 
\par \pard\plain \s1\sb480\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\fs36\lang2057\kerning28 \page {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267978}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554695}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378660}Chapter 3. The Moscow Electro-Mobile Component Repair Plant
{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267978}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554695}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378660}
\par \pard\plain \s2\sb360\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\i\f5\fs28\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267979}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554696}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378661}Brief history of the plant{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267979}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554696}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378661}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
The plant was established in 1940 by the Moscow metro to reconstruct and renovate carriages and carry out repairs. In 1936-1937 the first Moscow metro stations were built and a repair workshop was organised. On the place where the Popugai electrical depot
  is now situated repair workshops were opened, which were soon reorganised into the electro-mobile rolling stock repair plant \endash  Remet.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
During the Second World War the plant, apart from the repair of electric trains, produced some other items for the Russian army. Several workshops were specialised exactly on production for the army. In 1945 the plant began to fulfil the repair of carriag
es of different types, one of the workshops at the same time was producing parts for agricultural purposes.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar In research for the history of the plant I made a lot of efforts with low results. A lot of materials were destroyed by the state system of savi
ng materials, because they were not included in the list of materials which should be saved forever. 
\par Nevertheless I tried to find people who had worked in the plant at that time. Here is the evidence of one of the women, who came to work on the plant in 1946 and has worked in the plant for 50 years:
\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 There were a lot of good things. When I came to the plant, the managers usually said: \lquote Let\lquote s work, things will be better then\rquote 
. And we worked with such enthusiasm that I can not even express.  And we worked hard. The managers asked: \lquote The New Year is close, who will meet it on the carriages?\rquote 
. It meant that we should help the carriages workers to meet the plan. I was employed as a clerk in the personnel department. But at the end of the month, when the workshop 
had to meet the target, all the clerks helped the workers: washed the carriages from inside, cleaned the windows, made everything, apart from the bottom of the carriage. It was a very happy time. If the workers put on some parts in the trolley and I passe
d through the workshop, I always helped the workers.
\par \pard \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
At the very beginning we were told that we should work and work and work. First of all work, then personal life. The discipline was very strict in the plant, we came to work early, we were afraid to be fired. And everybody around was saying that work 
\endash  the speciality \endash  is the main thing for the future. I was very hard working and if I began my life from the beginning, I think that I would repeat it in the same way.
\par The territory around the plant was very dirty, it was oil and fuel on the land. It was a nightmare. So we organised special {\i subbotniks} (Saturday work). and built a garden near the plant\lquote s building. One of the leaders told the employees: 
\lquote I will build everything for you\rquote . And after some time passed he built a really beautiful fountain. There were different coloured lights in it and it wa
s made so that the water was also coloured. It was fantastic. We were young and we were happy.
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 In 1948 the plant began to make wheel-pairs for new trains for the Mos
cow metro. It was the only plant-supplier of the wheel-pairs for the plants which produced carriages for the metro. Since 1948 the plant has fulfilled three kinds of tasks: maintenance repair of the electric trains, capital repair (full renovation) of the
 electric trains and the forming of wheel-pairs for the electric trains.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar From the beginning of its existence the plant serviced all twelve metro systems in the Soviet Union.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar In 1974 the plant began to build a new territory in order to increase its productio
n capacity. The new territory was built in the suburbs of Moscow according to plans developed by the institute Transeletroproject. Thus, the plant since that time has consisted of two territories: Popugai in the centre of Moscow and Kukino in the Moscow s
uburbs.
\par All the time, from the beginning of the plant\rquote 
s existence, there were difficulties with fulfilling the plan. And even if it was the only plant in the former USSR which repaired the electric trains, nevertheless, the plant was in a very difficult financial and economical situation at the end of 80-s.:

\par \pard\plain \s18\qj\li245\sb60\sa60\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\fs22\lang2057 The low-prestige status of the enterprise, which belonged to the system of the Ministry of Road Communications (MPS) influenced the life of the plant\rquote 
s employees, and in the first place their wages, which were traditionally lower than in the whole Moscow metro. (Farewell poverty. How the repair plant became a profitable enterprise. Newspaper {\i Vek}, 15.11.05.1995.) 
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Thus, by the beginning of the 90-s the plant was in a very difficult situation and it could be excluded from the list of the profitable Soviet enterprises.
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267980}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554697}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378662}Elections{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267980}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554697}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378662}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
At the end of the 80-s and the beginning of the 90-s in Russia there was further development of the democratic processes. One of the main features of that era was elections of the senior managers of enterprises. As in some other places, in the Remet plant
 democratic elections were organised. It is interesting to note that in the period preceding the elections within the plant 4 or 5 directors were changed. They governed for 2 - 3 years
 and disappeared, without any noticeable changes within the enterprise. The plant was in a difficult financial and economic position, and there were not many people who wanted to govern it.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
The new General Director of the enterprise was elected by the collective of the plant in 1990 on the base of an open competition. There were 3 applicants, but several days before the elections the remaining two candidates changed their minds and withdrew 
their applications. So the new director was the only candidate and 
he won the election. Very soon after the election, he worked out a program to develop his independence from the metro authorities and leasing seemed to be the preferable way out. The documents was drawn up and the plant sent them to the appropriate commit
tee in Moscow. Thus, from April 1990 the plant became leasing enterprise, although the decision was made very slowly and only at the beginning 1992 were the documents about the leasehold finally signed.
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267981}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554698}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378663}Leasehold{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267981}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554698}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378663}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Since Perestroika began in Russia there was 
a shift towards privatisation. But real privatisation was possible only after 1992, when the Russian president signed several degrees about privatisation. At the beginning of the 90-s the only way for further independence was leasing. The General Director
 of the plant realised that for the plant it would be more profitable to be privatised. But the metro\rquote s authorities didn\rquote 
t give him permission to do so. In conditions of constant struggle, the plant was allowed to lease all its equipment and buildings, but 
without the right subsequently to buy. The general conditions of agreement were that the plant would be on lease for 5 years. A document was signed with the Moscow Metro Administration about amortisation of the equipment and planned repair of the carriage
s.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
The first steps of the leasing enterprise were very difficult. The former authorities were not satisfied with the situation, when the plant was not subordinated to them. They tried to put up every obstacle to complicate the life of the plant. They had be
en used to considering the plant as their property and they could not understand that the plant now acts as an independent unit. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
In fact, the lease meant for the plant that it should find ways to operate in the new market conditions without any help from the metro authorities. The situation with equipment on the plant was quite difficult: up to 80 % of it was worn out. The main goo
d thing for the plant was that with the help of lease the plant escaped from the dictatorship of the metro authorities. If befo
re the plant always had to consult with them about everything, now it received much more freedom, for example in formation its own payment system, clients\rquote 
 connections and soon. At the same time, although all employees automatically became leaseholders, t
here were no any substantial changes in their rights. In fact not one of the workers could explain the differences between the modern situation and the past.
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267982}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554699}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378664}The governing body{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267982}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554699}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378664}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 Since the plant began working on lease, the main management team was the governing body:
\par \pard \qj\li720\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 1. General Director
\par 2. Vice Director on Production in Popugai area
\par 3. Vice-Direction on Production in Kukino area.
\par 4. Head of the Planning-Production Department.
\par 5. Vice-Director on Economic Questions.
\par 6. Lawyer
\par 7. Head of the Marketing Department.
\par \pard \qj\li720\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 8. Head of the Department of the External Co-operation.
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar As we can see, the governing body consisted of the upper leaders. There were not any shop chiefs at all. This situation caused some problems, when the most urgent production questions were not resol
ved or had been delayed. Besides, in the governing body there were not any women, although there are a lot of women at the plant.
\par The governing body had a meeting once a month and resolved the most difficult industrial questions, but the governing body was not a harmonious team. The differences in views and positions were inspired by the general manager himself, who used the policy 
of \lquote divide and rule\rquote . 
\par From October 1992 the General Director created the position of First Vice-Director, who became responsib
le for all questions regarding production. The General Director himself has responsible since then for international activity, for searching for new partners and clients all over the world. 
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267983}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554700}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378665}The technology{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267983}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554700}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378665}

\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The technology at the plant is very old. Some of the equipment dates from the late 40-s. The majority of the equipment \endash  up to 70 % \endash 
 was bought in the early 70s. The technology of the repair of carriages had been worked out in the 50s, and most of the technological processes were written in the middle 70s. 
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267984}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554701}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378666}The repair{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267984}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554701}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378666}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
The repair of carriages was fulfilled by both the metro depots and the Remet plant. The small defects had to be repaired in the depots. More serious repair was done on the plant. After a certain amount of mileage all the carriages running on the metro had
 to pass through repair in the plant. Those vehicles which had been running on the rails for many years had to pass through capital repair (full repair). The rest of the vehicles had to pass through maintenance repair.
\par \pard\plain \s2\sb360\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\i\f5\fs28\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267985}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554702}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378667}Economic situation of the plant up to the end of 1993{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267985}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554702}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378667}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
As in the Soviet period, the main production of the plant was the repair of carriages for the Moscow metro. Sometimes there were several orders from the other metros in the former USSR - Tbilisi metro, Leningrad and so on. There was some kind of co-operat
ion between the Plant and other sites in the former USSR.
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267986}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554703}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378668}Supply problems{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267986}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554703}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378668}

\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 In the former USSR the whole supply system was organised through the special metro service which was called{\b  }MTS (Material-Technical Supply). For each en
terprise in the metro system there was a special quota. The plant had 40% quota for supplying material from the MTS system. Thus, in the past this service provided some minimum of parts needed by the plant. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar By the beginning of the 90s the situation had considerably changed. Moscow metro authorities considered the plant\rquote 
s desire to work on lease as an opposition to the whole metro system and they did not want to provide the plant with the parts through the previous system of MTS. Thus, in 1990, for example, there was non-delivery of 100 positions from the MTS system.

\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Besides the agreement with the MTS system, the plant had commercial agreements with different plants within USSR about the deliveries different parts. By the time the plant{\fs28  }was transferred to {\i arenda}{
\fs28  }
(leasehold) in 1990, there was a disruption of the whole system of economic connections between different parts of the former USSR. The whole system of centralised supplies from the different regions was collapsing and a lot of connections were cut of
f because of the collapse of the former USSR. From the following Diagram it is clear how supply provision for the plant was organised from the different parts of the former USSR: 
\par \pard \qr\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\pvpara\posy0\dxfrtext180\dfrmtxtx180\dfrmtxty0 Diagram 3.1.
\par \pard \qc\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar\pvpara\posy0\dxfrtext180\dfrmtxtx180\dfrmtxty0 {\b\fs28 Extraction from the range of suppliers:
\par }\pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
\par \trowd \trgaph108\trleft90\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr
\brdrs\brdrw15 \cellx1159\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cellx3645\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \cellx7291 \pard 
\s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl {\b N}{\b\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl {\b parts name }{\b\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl {\b Supplier}{\b\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\b\f133 \row }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft90
\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cellx1159\clbrdrt
\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cellx3645\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \cellx7291 \pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 1.{
\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl axles{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl Dnepropetrovsk, Ukraine{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 2.{\f133 \cell 
}\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl wheels semi-finished products{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl Nizhnyi Tagil{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 3.{\f133 \cell 
}\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl cogwheel{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl Saint-Petersburg{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 4.{\f133 \cell }\pard 
\s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl box-trees{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl Tver{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 5.{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 
bearing N 123{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl Vinnitsa, Ukraine{\f133 \cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\trowd \trgaph108\trleft90\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrr
\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cellx1159\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 
\cellx3645\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \cellx7291 \pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 6.{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl frames{\f133 \cell }\pard 
\s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl Moscow Mechanical Plant\cell \pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 \row \pard \qc\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar {\fs28 
\par }\pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar Thus, at the beginning of the 90s the plant was fac
ed with a lot of difficulties with the supply of materials and it had to make quick decisions for resolving this problem: to look for new partners or to arrange new agreements with the old ones which suddenly turned out to be abroad. It could not be done 
overnight. 
\par \pard \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar 
The external difficulties were supplemented by internal ones: up to March 1993 the enterprise had no marketing department and its role was fulfilled by the Department of External Co-operation. But its work had a lot of imperfections; people had 
no knowledge of how to work in the new conditions, there was a lack of information about the new laws and regulations and a lack of qualified specialists.
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267987}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554704}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378669}Relations with the other depots{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267987}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554704}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378669}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
First of all the depots and the plant were subordinated to the metro authorities, and were part of the same system. The depots maintained the vehicles and if they were not able to repair it, the vehicle was transferred to the plant for more serious repair
. The MTS system which supplied the plant provided up to 40 % of the p
arts needed by the depots. The rest of the parts the plant as well as depots had to find out themselves. Thus, the depots and the plant had constant problems with spare parts, and were looking for any other ways of coping with this problem. One of the way
s of resolving these problems was the system of {\i\ul informal exchanging}
 of parts between the plant and different depots. Usually the shop chief or the vice shop chief called to the nearest depots in case of lack of some parts in order to find the parts needed. 
People from the depots used to do the same things if they had a lack of parts for repair. 
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267988}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554705}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378670}Crises of non-payments{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267988}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554705}
{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378670}
\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The situation with supply problems was aggravated by the crises of non-payments, which began in 1992. A lot of enterprises had no cash. Some of the plant\rquote 
s clients was among them. They didn\rquote t pay for the work fulfilled. As a result the plant had difficulties with cash as well. This crisis touched the relationship between the Moscow metro authorities and the plant. There was tension in their rela
tions, because the plant fulfilled repair of carriages, but the metro authorities didn\rquote t pay for it, because of the difficulties with getting cash. A lack of cash immediately influenced the problems with people.
\par \pard\plain \s3\qj\sb240\sa120\sl480\slmult1\keepn\widctlpar \b\f5\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart _Toc404267989}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc407554706}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc408378671}Staff problems{\*\bkmkend _Toc404267989}{\*\bkmkend _Toc407554706}{\*\bkmkend _Toc408378671}

\par \pard\plain \qj\sa120\sl480\slmult1\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 The difficult situation in the plant influenced the bad situation with labour turnover. Let\rquote s take the year 1993 as an example.
\par \pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 
\par \trowd \trgaph108\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 
\cellx4374\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \cellx8748 \pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl The number of employees at the beginning of 1993{\f133 \cell }\pard \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl 1372{\f133 \cell 
}\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 {\f133 \row }\trowd \trgaph108\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrl
\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cellx4374\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw30 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw30 \cellx8748 \pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 Among them workers: {\f133 
\cell }1035\cell \pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl \f40\lang2057 \row \pard\plain \s34\qj\widctlpar \f40\lang2057 {\fs28 
